  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  October weather has arrived! Seasonal temperatures mean cool\n   afternoons and cooler mornings.\n\n-  Confidence is increasing for potential winter driving\n   conditions at mountain pass level Tuesday night into \n   Wednesday.\n   \nPrecipitation will continue this afternoon ending from northwest\nto southeast late afternoon into early evening. Observations from\naround the region also demonstrate snow levels are slowly\nlowering. Precipitation may linger in southwest MT as snow levels\nlower to or below mountain passes, 6000 feet or lower. Winter \nweather advisories remain in effect for this evenings rush hour to\nhighlight the potential for slick driving conditions over \nHomestake and MacDonald Passes. \n\nA northeasterly gradient continues over night as a cold Canadian\nair mass settles into central MT. If the gradient relaxes and\nclouds clear, efficient radiational cooling will allow for patchy\nto areas of valley fog or low stratus clouds to develop.\n\nSaturday through Monday will feel like October with seasonable\ntemperatures, cool afternoons and cold mornings. Sunday and Monday\nvalley fog and low stratus chances will contract back to more\ntypical valleys.\n\nThe models are coalescing around a solution that brings another\nweather system on Tuesday. Another round of valley rain and \nmountain snow is expected. Snow levels are forecast to lower below\nmountain passes overnight Tuesday and winter driving conditions \nmay be present at most regional passes Wednesday morning while \nonly Lost Trail Pass has the potential to receive snow for Tuesday\nmornings commute. Also Wednesday morning, along the Continental \nDivide the change over to snow in valleys may allow for minor \naccumulations on vegetation.",
